406→                           A conjecture of Erdős and Turán. Proved by Szemerédi <a href="#cite-Sz75-139" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Sz75','139'); return false;">[Sz75]</a>. The best known bounds are due to Kelley and Meka <a href="#cite-KeMe23-139" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('KeMe23','139'); return false;">[KeMe23]</a> for $k=3$ (with further slight improvements in <a href="#cite-BlSi23-139" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('BlSi23','139'); return false;">[BlSi23]</a>), Green and Tao <a href="#cite-GrTa17-139" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('GrTa17','139'); return false;">[GrTa17]</a> for $k=4$, and Leng, Sah, and Sawhney <a href="#cite-LSS24-139" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('LSS24','139'); return false;">[LSS24]</a> for $k\geq 5$.<br><br>In <a href="#cite-Er80-139" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Er80','139'); return false;">[Er80]</a> Erdős reports that <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hildegard_Rothe-Ille" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer ugc" target="_blank">Rothe-Ille</a> was given the problem of estimating $r_k(n)$ by Schur in the 1930s, and so 'perhaps Schur conjectured $r_k(N)=o(N)$ before Turán and myself'.<br><br>See also <a href="/3" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer ugc" target="_blank">[3]</a>.
